The error means the node entity's tables were never created. `KernelTestBase` installs nothing by itself: listing `node` in `$modules` only registers the module's services and hooks, it does not create storage. You have to call `installEntitySchema()` explicitly.

```php
<?php

namespace Drupal\Tests\my_module\Kernel;

use Drupal\KernelTests\KernelTestBase;
use Drupal\node\Entity\NodeType;
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\RunTestsInSeparateProcesses;

#[RunTestsInSeparateProcesses]
class MyModuleNodeTest extends KernelTestBase {

  /**
   * {@inheritdoc}
   */
  protected static $modules = [
    'system',
    'user',
    'field',
    'text',
    'filter',
    'node',
    'my_module',
  ];

  /**
   * {@inheritdoc}
   */
  protected function setUp(): void {
    parent::setUp();

    // Creates the {users} and {users_field_data} tables. Nodes have an author
    // reference, so this is required even if the test never touches users.
    $this->installEntitySchema('user');

    // Creates {node}, {node_field_data}, {node_revision}, etc. This is the
    // missing call that produces "table node_field_data does not exist".
    $this->installEntitySchema('node');

    // node.settings, field settings, and the fallback text format. Without
    // 'filter' installed, saving a body value fails on an unknown format.
    $this->installConfig(['field', 'node', 'filter']);

    // Only needed if the test exercises node access / grants. Omit otherwise.
    $this->installSchema('node', ['node_access']);

    // Node types are config entities, so they need no schema, but they do not
    // exist until created. Node::create() with an unknown bundle will fail.
    NodeType::create([
      'type' => 'article',
      'name' => 'Article',
    ])->save();
  }

}
```

A few things worth checking against your actual test:

- **`user` schema is not optional.** People often add `installEntitySchema('node')` alone and then hit a `users_field_data` error on the first `$node->save()`, because the `uid` base field resolves the author.
- **Bundle must exist.** `Node::create(['type' => 'article'])` throws if no `article` node type has been created. If `my_module` ships the bundle as config, add it to `installConfig(['my_module'])` instead of building it in the test.
- **`node_access`.** Drop that line unless you are testing access; it is the second most common "table does not exist" in node kernel tests, but installing it needlessly just slows setup.
- **Don't add `installSchema('system', ['sequences'])`.** That advice is all over older docs and Stack Exchange. The `sequences` table was removed in Drupal 10.2 and the call will fail on 10.2+/11.
- **If you need a body field**, `node_add_body_field()` is not run for you — call it on the node type, or use `installConfig()` on a module that provides the field config.

If you also see errors about `key_value` or `cachetags` after this, that is a separate symptom: `system` is missing from `$modules`.
